      "math_academy_id": "q-183328",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Describe the set $S={…,3,6,12,24,48,...}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take ${a}_{1}=6$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S.$*\n\n$$\nS={{{field-1}}\\,:\\,n\\in {{field-2}}}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n…,\\,3,\\,6,\\,12,\\,24,\\,48,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a two-way infinite geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{12} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting  ${a}_{1}=6,$ the $n$th term ${a}_{n}$ of our sequence for $n\\in Z$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot {2}^{n}\\cdot {2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2}\\cdot {2}^{n} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS={3\\cdot {2}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in Z}\n$$",
